<XuiHtmlElement>
<Properties>
<Id>XuiHtmlElement1</Id>
<Width>544.000000</Width>
<Height>312.000000</Height>
<Position>24.000000,56.000000,0.000000</Position>
<Text>&lt;font color="#000000" size=11&gt;

While &lt;i&gt;&lt;b&gt;Figures&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/i&gt; themselves are not actually controls, they can be used to create the visual look and feel of a control.
There is a palette of shapes you can use as a base, or you can start drawing one by hand.

&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;font size=8&gt;
&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td width="4%"/&gt;
&lt;td width="3%" bgcolor="#808080"/&gt;
&lt;td width="86%" bgcolor="#808080"&gt;

&lt;font size=5&gt;
&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="#FFFFFF" size=10&gt;


&lt;i&gt;&lt;b&gt;Figures&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/i&gt; can be filled will a solid color, linear / radial gradient, or texture... and they support an optional stroke with size and color.
You can also use the 'View &gt; Multisampling' menu option to render anti-aliased.

&lt;font size=5&gt;
&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;/font&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td width="3%" bgcolor="#808080"/&gt;
&lt;td width="4%"/&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
</Text>
</Properties>
</XuiHtmlElement>
